{%- doc -%}
  A title with its accent words marked, ready to print inside whatever element the
  caller wants.

  Treat House draws part of a name in purple. Where a merchant is typing the words
  they mark them with `<em>` in the editor's own toolbar; where the words come from
  a product or a collection there is no toolbar, so the accent is a metafield
  instead — `custom.title_accent`, holding the words to colour, spelled as they
  appear in the title.

  This is the second half of that bargain, and it lives here because the rule is now
  read in more than one place: the [_ui-heading] block on a product or collection
  page, and the line items in the cart. One copy, so a title cannot come out purple
  in the drawer and black on the page.

  The title is escaped before the accent is marked, so the `<em>` is the only markup
  in the string and a product called "Cookies & Cream" still prints as one.

  The match is literal and case-sensitive. A merchant writes the accent by copying
  it out of the title, so a loose match would only ever surprise them — "Bar"
  colouring the "Bar" inside "Barrington" is not a thing anyone asked for. Every
  occurrence is marked, which is what a title that says the word twice wants.

  Nothing to mark and it prints the escaped title, which is what a product without
  the metafield should look like.

  @param {string} title - The title to print.
  @param {string} accent - The words within it to mark. Usually a metafield.

  @example
  <h1>{% render 'ui-title-accent', title: product.title, accent: product.metafields.custom.title_accent %}</h1>
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  # A metafield object is not a string. `.value` is what a single-line text field
  # hands back; passing the object itself and asking it to escape renders nothing.
  assign accent_value = accent.value | default: accent

  # Through [strip-hidden-words], because this snippet is the one home a product
  # name has: the same name is printed from here on the product page, in the cart
  # drawer and in the heading blocks. Taking the code off in one of those would
  # have meant leaving it standing in the others.
  capture title_shown
    render 'strip-hidden-words', title: title
  endcapture

  assign title_text = title_shown | strip | escape
  assign accent_text = accent_value | escape

  if accent_text != blank and title_text contains accent_text
    assign marked = '<em>' | append: accent_text | append: '</em>'
    assign title_text = title_text | replace: accent_text, marked
  endif

  echo title_text
-%}
